{#-
======================================================================
Rust.stg.jinja — minijinja port of the dbt ANTLR fork's `Rust.stg`
(tool/resources/org/antlr/v4/tool/templates/codegen/Rust/Rust.stg).

STG -> Jinja mapping conventions:

  <x.y>                          ->  {{ x.y }}
  <if(c)> ... <elseif(d)> ... <else> ... <endif>
                                 ->  {% if c %} ... {% elif d %} ... {% else %} ... {% endif %}
  <list:{x | body}; separator="s"> -> {% for x in list %}body{{ "s" if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}
  <list; separator=", ", wrap, anchor>
                                 ->  {{ list | wrap_list(", ", <start-col>, "<indent>", <anchor-col>) }}
                                     (replicates ST4 AutoIndentWriter wrapping at the
                                      ANTLR line width of 72 columns)
  <list:{x | body}; wrap>        ->  {% for x in list %}{{ ST_WRAP }}body{% endfor %}
                                     (wrap without anchor: the mark becomes a newline
                                      plus the line's indentation at column >= 72)
  <RustTypeMap.(t)>              ->  {{ rust_type(t) }}
  <rustTypeInitMap.(t)>          ->  {{ rust_type_init(t) }}
  <name; format="cap">           ->  {{ name | cap }}
  <Name(args)>                   ->  {{ Name(args) }}  (macro call)

Whitespace notes (matching ST4 behavior):

  - The environment enables trim_blocks + lstrip_blocks, which matches ST4's
    stripping of the newline after <if>/<else>/<endif> and of the whitespace
    before those tags.
  - A line holding only an expression that renders empty disappears in ST4
    (indent + newline are suppressed); here those spots are written as
    {% if value %}...{% endif %} guards.
  - ST4 indents every non-empty line of a value rendered on an indented line
    (AutoIndentWriter INDENT); here the call sites apply the st_indent filter.
  - ST4 scopes attributes dynamically, so nested templates see the enclosing
    template's arguments (for example `namedActions`, `file`). Jinja macros
    do not: the affected macros take those values as extra trailing
    arguments.
  - Escaped STG delimiters: \< and \> in Rust.stg are plain < and > here.

Template names, argument lists, and relative order mirror the Rust.stg
templates they port. The lexer subset covers milestones 4.1/4.2; the
parser subset (milestone 4.3) covers the CSV.g4 construct space (plain
rules, EBNF closures, LL(1) choices, context structs/getters).

Parser-specific notes:

  - The `self()` template is kept under its original name: unlike older
    minijinja versions, 2.24 accepts `self` as a macro name (verified by
    probe).
  - ST4 renders model objects through templates selected by class name
    (OutputModelWalker); here the `Op`/`Getter`/`Decl` dispatcher macros
    switch on the model `kind` tag instead. Note that in ST4,
    `parser.funcs` resolves to the raw *model* objects (the `parser`
    formal argument), while `<funcs>` renders walked templates; here both
    read the same model data, with macros playing the walker's role.
  - The parser ATN is emitted in the vendored packed format: the parser
    file uses `PackedSerializedATN` (packed u32 words decoded by
    `dbt_antlr_runtime::atn_packed_deserializer::PackedATNDeserializer`) in place
    of Rust.stg's `SerializedATN` (Java i32 words + `ATNDeserializer`),
    and imports the packed deserializer instead of the Java-word one.
  - `cases` is ported without the `wrap` option (72-column wrapping of
    long match arms is not needed by the covered grammars).
  - Labels/action chunks are rejected by the factory, so label-aware
    branches of `LabelsAssign`, `InvokeRule` argument chunks, and
    `AltLabelStructDecl` are not exercised yet.
  - Milestone 4.4 adds left-recursive rules: `LeftRecursiveRuleFunction`,
    `SemPred` (the `recRuleAltPredicate` form), the `recRule*` macros, and
    the `InvokeRule` `_rec` precedence suffix.
  - Milestone 4.5 adds the `ListenerFile`/`BaseListenerFile`/`VisitorFile`/
    `BaseVisitorFile` templates; the root dispatch selects them via the
    top-level `kind` tag of the render context (the `Op` dispatcher's
    class-name convention applied to the four file models, which share the
    `(file, header, namedActions)` formal arguments). The dead STG
    `ListenerDispatchMethod`/`VisitorDispatchMethod` templates (unreferenced
    in Rust.stg) are not ported.
======================================================================
-#}
